    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Looking back over your life, can you recall any specific periods, perhaps lasting a few months, where you felt this same heavy, hopeless way, even if you managed to push through it at the time?",
      "targeted_symptom": "depressive_episode_duration",
      "patient_response": "Well... okay, if I really sit and try to trace it back... yeah. Yeah, I can pinpoint it, actually. It hasn't just been a few months. It’s been... gosh, it’s been about a year and two months now. I know that because it started a little bit after my husband’s injury. He got hurt at his job, and it was this whole big thing with worker's comp and him being laid up, and I had to take on extra shifts. I was doing all the heavy lifting, literally, at work, carrying rolls of carpet up three flights of stairs by myself because my partner called out, and then coming home to try and take care of him. And at first, you know, I thought I was just exhausted. Like, bone-tired from the physical labor and the stress of the bills piling up on the kitchen counter. I remember sitting at the table, looking at the electric bill, and just crying, which isn't like me. I’m the one who fixes things. I lay the floor, I make a solid foundation, you know? But then... it just never lifted. Even when he started physical therapy and things got a tiny bit easier at home, this... this darkness just stayed. It settled in right around then, about fourteen months ago, and it’s just been this constant, heavy weight pulling me down every single day since. I kept thinking, 'Okay, next week I'll feel better, next week I'll have energy,' but it just kept stretching on and on, month after month, until here we are, over a year later, and I just can't see the light anymore.",
