      "doctor_question": "During these past fourteen months, have there been any periods of at least two weeks where you felt so slowed down that other people might have noticed you moving or speaking much more sluggishly than usual, or conversely, felt so agitated that you couldn't sit still?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Well, I... I don't think so? I mean, I'm always tired, like I said, bone-tired. When you're hauling boxes of ceramic tile out of a van and mixing thinset all day, you're not exactly moving like a ballerina when you get home, you know? My knees ache, my back aches, and sometimes it takes me a minute just to stand up straight after I've been kneeling for hours. So, yeah, I guess I move slow, but that's just the job. That's the physical toll of it. I've never had anyone at work pull me aside and say, 'Hey, you're moving like molasses today,' or anything like that. My crew, they know I work hard. I've always been a hard worker. Even with this... this heavy blanket over me, I still try to keep up the pace. And as for being agitated or not being able to sit still... no, not really. If anything, all I want to do is sit still. Or lay down. I just want to lay down and not have to move or think or do anything at all. When I get home, I just kind of collapse on the couch, and my husband—bless him, he tries—he'll ask me about my day, and I just... I don't even have the energy to talk. I just kind of stare at the TV without really watching it. So, no, no pacing around or feeling like I have a motor running inside me. It's more like the motor just completely died, and I'm just this heavy, empty shell trying to drag myself through the day. I don't think anyone would say I'm moving unusually slow for what I do, just... normal tired from the work, even if inside it feels like I'm walking through wet concrete.",
